.he               Ken Kahn's 6.871 Term Paper      Page  
.ad
.sp
.ce
Introduction
     The process of hand coding medical frames is slow
and tedious.  Problems of coordinating many workers
with many different styles and opinions as to how best
construct a frame are very serious hinderances to
developing a very large medical knowledge data base.
An obvious solution to this problem is to automate
the process of writing frames.  
This paper will informally discuss a few ideas
as how this might be done.
     Another motivation for studying the learning of
medical knowledge is that the understanding of the
development of the knowledge in doctors may lead
to a better understanding of its content and structure.
Piaget has been very successful in this regard.  The
development of medical knowledge takes place over a
longer time and is more complex than the systems that
Piaget studies however I hope to show that it is
plausible that such research is feasable.
.sp
.ce
Designing Meta-frames
     One idea would be to design a few models of what
medical frames look like.  A disease meta-frame might
specify that the usual frame has a few principal parts,
many "findings" and their associated likelihoods,
a few differential diagnosis pointers,
a few casual explanations, a few prerequistes, and
other pointer types.  Also the relations between these
concepts might be included.  The system would work
like a usual frame system.  Quickly the system would
form an hypothesis as to whether it is learning about
a disease, a clinical finding, a laboratory finding,
complicated symptoms, etc..  The meta-frame would include
advise about how to fill in an instantiation of the
frame-type and
how to characterize the problem so that more particular advise becomes
available.  Either as it goes, or afterwards, the system
would ask rather directed questions trying to fill in its
slots.  The system would need to make heavy use of a
discrepancy subsystem so that consistency of the knowledge
is high. 
.sp
.ce
Learning by Analogy
     Another mechanism may be necessary to have a useful
system and that is the notion of this piece of knowledge
is structurally similar to one that is already know.  In other
words, suppose the system knew a lot about AGN and was
learning about the Nephrotic Syndrome.  Then perhaps
in addition to using the meta-frame for constructing
disease frames, the system could expect a similar structure
for the Nephrotic Syndrome as AGN.  It would be using
AGN as a prototype and be reasoning by example.
.sp
.ce
A Partial Solution
     One may argue and probably rigthfully so, that this is
too far off to be very interesting to someone trying to
mechanize medical knowledge with the present state of the art.
But only part of the system might be useful and more practical
to implement.  Something that might be worth thinking about
today is a system that could learn if spoon feed. Basically
the system would be conversational and wait for knowledge
for the user, presumably a doctor. The system would be able
to ask questions to fill in slots and to find out where to
put things that are tricky.  This partial system would
still require a good parser but less need be known about
frame writing by the designers. The following is a typical
conversation with our hypothetical system:
.sp
.indent 4
Doctor: Hello I'm going to teach you about AGN today.
.br
Computer: Okay what is AGN?
.br
(Here the system simply asks what frame-type the user
is talking about.)
.br
Doctor: AGN is a kidney disease.
.br
Computer: Good, tell me please, what are the principal
parts of this disease.
.br
(There is a standard scenario of asking for such things
as principal parts, time and duration of signs and
symptoms, things that are likely to be confused with
the disease, etc.  This is associated with the frame-type
frame and is similar to the notion of scenario in OWL.
These questions are only asked if the user doesn't
say anything.)
.br
Doctor: A prior streptococcal infection, edema, and
glomerulitis.
.br
Computer: I know about streptococcal infections, is
there anything special about the streptococcal
infection that occurs in AGN?
.br
(A reasonable default strategy in lieu of any other
advise is to go through the principle parts at this
point one at a time storing the information as that
principal part or that principal part in AGN.  First it
sees if its streptococcal infection frame is happy,
that is the system knows of no holes in its knowledge about
streptococcal infections. In this scenario we assume
the system had previously learned about streptococcal
infections but has no parasites (in Falhman's sense of parasitic
frames) to that frame of the
type streptococcal infection except modified by only
occuring as part of AGN.  So it tries to construct
such a parasite.)
.br
Doctor: Well, the temperature range is narrower, about
99 degrees to 104 degrees.  The culture is beta-hemalytic.
.br
Computer: Anything else?
.br
(Supposedly the streptococcal infection frame has statements
about temperature ranges and cultures so the construction of
the parasite is not too difficult.  As a general rule the
system asks is there anything more before going on to the next
one to lessen the possibility that the doctor forgot something
important.)
.br
Doctor: Nothing important.
.br
Computer: You mentioned earlier that edema is a principal
part of AGN, I don't think I know anything about edema.
.br
(Here we suppose that upon searching that no general edema
frame was found.)
.br
Doctor: The edema in AGN is usually periorbital.
.br
Computer: Excuse me, what does "periorbital" mean?
.br
(Rather than let things it doesn't know get by the system
asks immediately what things mean.)
.br
Doctor: That it is of the eyes.
.br
Computer: Please go on, you were saying that the edema
occuring in AGN is usually of the eyes.
.br
Doctor: Yes, it is usually worst upon wakening and is
symmetric. Sometimes there is pedal edema in AGN.
.br
Computer:  I think I understand but what is edema, is
it a laboratory finding, a symptom, a disease or what?
.br
(The system has to interrupt occasionally when something
it thinks is essential is missing in this case what kind
of thing edema is.  The system prompts the doctor by giving
the most common things a principal part can be kinds of.)
.br
Doctor:  It's a symptom that manifests itself by swelling
that is indentable.
.br
(The system often does simple deductions. For example,
in this case, suppose a slot for symptoms is who the agent
is that can observe the symptom.  The system would become
too much of a pain if it asked "simple" questions all the
time so it deduces from knowledge about swelling and indenting
that it can be observed by the patient or doctor.)
.br
Computer: Is there anything else about edema occurring in
AGN that might be important?
.br
Doctor: Can't think of anything.
.br
Computer: Is there anything special about glomerulitis
when it occurs in AGN?
.br
Doctor: No.
.br
Computer:  Please tell me about the time course of AGN,
I assume that the streptococcal infection, edema, glomerulitis
and whatever other findings that occur in AGN (which we will
get to in a minute) don't all begin and end together.
.br
(Here the top level scenario takes over and asks about
time courses, the order of the questions is not essential.)
.sp
.indent 0
     And it goes on and on.  Aside from the natural language
aspect, the system knows a fair amount about what a disease
description should look like, and uses common sense knowledge
heavily to avoid asking stupid questions and not assumming
inconsistencies.  Notice that the problem of where to put the
knowledge is not so difficult since the context tells you that
this is streptococcal infection in AGN and that's where the 
knowledge goes.  Notice that this is not the case in reading 
medical  literature necessarily.  With books you have
the added burden of deciding what is worth indexing what is
and what is redundant or immaterial.
Consistent representations in provided by
the parser.  The question of the feasability of such a parser
is up in the air right now.
.sp
.ce
Learning from Experience
     Hopefully, the system could learn from experience.  The
system could summarize statistics based on cases it handled
and change likelihoods, typical-values, typical-ranges, etc.
appropriately.  It could also remember odd cases and add
to the appropriate frame that sometimes or rarely particular
things occur with this frame.       
     I am not proposing that such a system be implemented
because the present state of knowledge of frame writing
is too crude.  However, in a couple years with the help
of a decent parser this project doesn't seem too far
fetched.  Without more experience it is difficult to
decide how many meta-frames are necessary, what exactly
they should know, and how well incoming knowledge should
fit these frames.  
.sp
.ce
"Short-term" Research Methods
     One can do more than just wait for experience as
we write more frames, that is we could study medical
students and neophyte doctors as they learn about new
things.  Basically a long term protocol analysis
combined with an understanding of what the subject has
been studying between protocols would provide the
necessary information.  A shorter term experiment
might be to do a protocol analysis of a learning
doctor as he learns rather than do diagnosis.  The
subject is either told statement by statement facts
about a disease that he or she knows little about.
The subject is required to explain what he or she
thinks it means.  This would be similar to CPC
mode protocols.  Similarly one could tell the
subject that he or she can ask whatever they like
about a disease they know little about and are somehow
tested latter, perhaps by a diagnosis protocol.
